eful for the final pair in the winning hand. Also, having a pair of B4s might help in forming a pung if they get another B4. However, there are still 2 B4s left in the wall (since unshown tiles show B4:4, and they have 2, so 4-2=2 left). So the chance of getting another B4 is possible, but not high.\n\n3. **Discard B7**: They have one B7. Discarding it might be safe, but if they can form a B7, B8, B9 chow if they get B9. But B9 is in the previous player's melds as a discard (they played B9 after Peng F2). So if B9 is already out, then they can't form that chow. Wait, the previous player's melds have a B9 in their discard? Wait, the previous player's game history is Play B6, Peng F2, Play B9. So B9 is already discarded. So B9 is out, so the user can't form a B7-B8-B9 chow. Therefore, B7 and B8 are just single tiles. Discarding B7 might be safe.\n\n4. **Discard T3**: They have two T3s. Keeping a pair is good. Discarding one T3 might not be ideal unless they can form a chow with T2, T3, T4. But they don't have T4. So T3 is part of a pair. Discarding one would leave a single T3, which might not be helpful.\n\n5. **Discard W6**: They have one W6. If they discard it, they lose the possibility of forming a W5, W6, W7 chow if they get W7. But they don't have W7. Wait, their hand has W5, W6, W8. So W5 and W6 could be part of a chow if they get W4 or W7. But without those, it's not helpful. Discarding W6 might be safe.\n\n6. **Discard T7**: They have two T7s. Discarding one would leave a single T7. Since B9 is out, they can't form a T7, T8, T9 chow. So maybe it's safe to discard.\n\n7. **Discard W5**: They have one W5. If they discard it, they lose the possibility of a W5, W6, W7 chow if they get W7. But again, they don't have W7. So maybe safe to discard.\n\n8. **Discard B3**: They have one B3. If they discard it, they can't form a B3, B4, B5 chow if they get B4 or B5. But they have B4. If they have B3 and B4, getting B5 would complete the chow. However, they only have one B