tween discarding a tile that's part of a potential chow (B4) or a single tile (W6). \n\nSince the game is about building towards a winning hand, it's better to keep the tiles that can form melds. Therefore, discarding W6 is better because it's not part of any potential chow, whereas B4 could be part of one. \n\nTherefore, the best move is to play W6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The player has a concealed hand with multiple potential melds. Discarding W6, a single tile not part of any sequence, is the safest move to avoid aiding opponents while preserving key tiles for future melds.\n\nReason: W6 is a single tile with no potential to form a chow or pung in the current hand. Discarding it maintains flexibility for developing sequences in other suits (e.g., T7-T8-T9 or B3-B4-B5). Other options like T3/T7 risk breaking pairs or aiding opponents' chows.\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
