ne, we have one left. Not helpful unless we can form a chow.\n\n3. Play F4: Discard F4. F4 is a single tile. Not part of any meld. Discarding it would free up a tile.\n\n4. Play B7: Discard B7. B7 is a single tile. If we need to form a chow with B7-B8-B9, but we need B9. If we discard B7, maybe we can't form that.\n\n5. Play J1: Discard J1. J1 is a single tile. Not part of any meld. Discarding it would be safe.\n\n6. Play T3: Discard T3. T3 is part of two copies. If we discard one, we still have one left. Maybe useful for a pair or chow.\n\n7. Play T7: Discard T7. Similar to T3. Two copies. Discard one, keep one.\n\n8. Play W5: Discard W5. Single tile. Not part of any meld.\n\n9. Play B3: Discard B3. Single tile. Not part of any meld.\n\n10. Play T2: Discard T2. T2 is part of a single tile. If we need to form a chow with T2-T3-T4, but we need T4. Discarding T2 would prevent that.\n\n11. Play B8: Discard B8. Single tile. If we need B7-B8-B9, but need B9.\n\nNow, considering the possible melds, maybe keeping the pairs is better. For example, T3 (two), T7 (two), B4 (two). If I discard one of these, I lose a potential pair. So maybe it's better to keep the pairs and try to form other melds.\n\nLooking at the hand, if I can form a pair of B4 and then form chows. For example, if I discard a non-pair tile like F4 or J1. Let's see:\n\nIf I discard F4, then my hand has T2, T3 (two), T7 (two), T9, B3, B4 (two), B7, B8, J1, W5. The pair is B4. Then, possible chows: T3-T4 (need T4), B3-B4-B5 (need B5), B7-B8-B9 (need B9). Not helpful.\n\nIf I discard J1, then the hand has T2, T3 (two), T7 (two), T9, B3, B4 (two), B7, B8, F4, W5. Pair is B4. Same as above.\n\nIf I discard W5, then the hand has T2, T3 (two), T7 (two), T9, B3, B4 (two), B7, B8, F4, J1. Pair is B4. Still same.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard a single tile like B3 or B7, but they are part of possible chows. For example, B3 is part of B3-B4-B5. If I discard B3, then I can't form that chow. Similarly for B7.\n\nMaybe t