ut in the game history, I played J1. Wait, maybe the game history is the previous actions, but the current hand still has J1. That might be possible if I drew another J1 after playing. So if I discard J1 again, maybe it's a repeat, but I have to check if that's allowed. Since there are 2 J1 left in unshown, maybe it's safe to discard.\n\nPlay T3: I have two T3s. Discarding one could be okay. \n\nPlay T7: Two T7s. Discarding one might help, but if I can form a pair or pung, maybe keep it.\n\nPlay F3: I have one F3. There are 2 left in unshown. Discarding F3 could be an option.\n\nPlay B3: One B3. There are 4 left. Discarding it might not be bad.\n\nPlay T2: One T2. There are 3 left. Discarding T2.\n\nPlay B8: One B8. There are 4 left.\n\nNow, considering my hand structure. I have some possible combinations. Let's see:\n\n- T3 (two) could form a pair or pung. But only two, so if I draw another, maybe a pung. \n\n- T7 (two) same as T3.\n\n- B4 (two) could be a pair.\n\n- B3, B4, B5: maybe a chow if I have B3, B4, B5. Wait, I have B3 (1), B4 (2), B5 (1). So if I can get another B5, that would make a chow. But currently, it's not possible. So maybe not.\n\nLooking at possible scoring patterns. Since my melds are empty, I'm trying to form a concealed hand. Let's check if I can reach 8 points. \n\nPossible patterns:\n\n- Seven Pairs: I have T3 (2), T7 (2), B4 (2). If I can get another two pairs, but I have B3, B5, B7, B8, F3, J1. Each is single. So maybe not.\n\n- All Chows: If I can form four chows and a pair. Let's see. My current tiles are T2, T3, T3, T7, T7, T9, B3, B4, B4, B5, B7, B8, F3, J1. Wait, that's 14 tiles. Wait, no, the hand is 13 tiles. Wait, the hand tiles are [T2:1; T3:2; T7:2; T9:1; B3:1; B4:2; B5:1; B7:1; B8:1; F3:1; J1:1]. So total 13 tiles. Let me count:\n\nT2 (1), T3 (2), T7 (2), T9 (1) = 6 tiles.\n\nB3 (1), B4 (2), B5 (1), B7 (1), B8 (1) = 6 tiles.\n\nF3 (1), J1 (1) = 2 tiles. Total 14? Wait, no. Wait, the user lists them as counts. So T2:1 means one